Exercises Notes Attempt each problem without help. Open its hint only when stuck, then compare your reasoning with the adjacent solution. Original tasks retain their source IDs; supplementary practice is labelled. L3 equal counts — Unbounded matching blocks Source: ORIGINAL COURSE EXERCISE — Lecture 3 Regular Expression and Pumping Lemma.pdf, PDF pp. 36–37. Prove $L=\{0^n1^n\mid n\geq0\}$ is not regular. > [!tip]- Hint > Choose a word whose first p symbols are all zero. Describe every possible legal y, rather than choosing one. > [!success]- Solution > Step 1 — Assumption and witness. Assume regularity and let $p\geq1$ be its pumping length. Choose $w=0^p1^p\in L$, of length $2p\geq p$. > > Step 2 — All legal splits. Any $w=xyz$ with $|xy|\leq p$ and $|y|\geq1$ has $x=0^a$, $y=0^b$ and $z=0^{p-a-b}1^p$, where $a\geq0$, $b\geq1$, $a+b\leq p$. > > Step 3 — Contradiction. Pump twice: $xy^2z=0^{p+b}1^p$. Since $b>0$, the zero count exceeds the one count, so the word is outside $L$. This defeats every valid split and contradicts the lemma. Hence $L$ is not regular. The remaining zeros in z repair the source's restricted displayed split. L3 square lengths — Unary squares Source: ORIGINAL COURSE EXERCISE — Lecture 3 Regular Expression and Pumping Lemma.pdf, PDF pp. 38–39. Prove $S=\{0^{n^2}\mid n\geq0\}$ is not regular. > [!tip]- Hint > The pumped length must lie strictly between two consecutive squares. Bound the increase using the split conditions. > [!success]- Solution > Assume $S$ regular and let $p\geq1$ be a pumping length. Choose $w=0^{p^2}\in S$, with $|w|=p^2\geq p$. For any permitted split, write $y=0^b$ with $1\leq b\leq p$. Pump to $xy^2z$, whose length is $p^2+b$. > > Because $1\leq b\leq p$, > > $$p^2<p^2+b\leq p^2+p<(p+1)^2.$$ > > The last inequality follows from $(p+1)^2-(p^2+p)=p+1>0$. Consecutive integer squares leave no other square between them, so the new length is not a square. Every split fails for $i=2$, contradicting the lemma. Thus S is not regular. The upper bound supplies the missing part of the printed argument. L3 Q&A — What does the lemma establish? Source: ORIGINAL COURSE EXERCISE — Lecture 3 Regular Expression and Pumping Lemma.pdf, PDF p. 41. 1. If you cannot find a regex for a language, have you proved it nonregular? 2. Why do we pump long strings? 3. If all pumped versions of one string stay in L, is L regular? 4. Is $0^n1^n$ a regular expression? > [!tip]- Hint > Separate an existence claim about all machines from your search, and a theorem about every long word from one example. > [!success]- Solution > 1. No. Failure to construct one does not rule out every possible expression or machine. Supply a nonregularity proof. > 2. A length of at least $p$ forces $p+1$ visits among $p$ DFA states, so a nonempty loop exists within the first $p$ transitions. Short words lack this guarantee. > 3. No conclusion of regularity follows. One successful word and split do not meet the lemma's requirements for every sufficiently long member. Moreover the lemma is a necessary condition, not a provided converse regularity criterion. > 4. If $n$ is fixed, the specific finite word can be written as a regex by concatenating n zeros and n ones. If $n$ ranges over all nonnegative integers with the two counts constrained equal, $0^n1^n$ is not an ordinary regex in the course's grammar; the language it describes is nonregular by the first proof. Practice — Find the unjustified choice Source: SUPPLEMENTARY PRACTICE — proof diagnosis based on the supplied lemma. A student says: “Assume a pumping length p for the equal-count language. Choose $w=0^p1^p$, split $x=\varepsilon,y=0,z=0^{p-1}1^p$, and pump down. The new word is outside the language, so the language is not regular.” Identify the logical gap and repair it. > [!tip]- Hint > The chosen split is legal, but the theorem says some split can pump. What about the others? > [!success]- Solution > The student refutes only one split; another could satisfy the lemma. Repair by taking an arbitrary legal split, writing $y=0^b$ with $1\leq b\leq p$, and observing that pumping down produces $0^{p-b}1^p$. Since $b\geq1$, this leaves the language for every split. The same witness now supports a valid contradiction. Practice transfer — Repair a square-length inference Source: SUPPLEMENTARY PRACTICE — proof diagnosis based on the supplied lemma. A proof says “the new length is greater than $p^2$, so it is not a square.” Explain why this is invalid and provide the missing inequality for the lecture witness. > [!tip]- Hint > A number larger than p² might equal (p+1)². Show why the permitted increment cannot reach it. > [!success]- Solution > The lower bound does not rule out later squares; for example $(p+1)^2>p^2$ is itself a square. Here the loop length b obeys $b\leq p$, so $p^2+b\leq p^2+p<(p+1)^2$. Together with $b\geq1$, this traps the length strictly between consecutive squares. Both bounds are essential. T3 P1 — What one pumpable word does not show ORIGINAL COURSE EXERCISE: current Tutorial 3 PDF pp.1–2, P1.4–5; solution notebook cell 4. (4) If one long word pumps for every i≥0, does that prove regularity? (5) Why only discuss words of length at least p? P1.1–3 are in Unit 03. > [!tip]- Hint > Compare a single successful example with the lemma's quantifiers, and count visits rather than distinct states. > [!success]- Solution > 4. No. One word does not establish the necessary property for every sufficiently long language member, and the lemma is not a sufficient regularity criterion. > > 5. Take a p-state DFA. Reading the first p symbols gives p+1 visits, including the initial state, so at least two visits coincide. Their intervening nonempty input labels a loop with $|xy|\le p$. A shorter run need not repeat a state. T3 P4 — Order and ownership ORIGINAL COURSE EXERCISE: Tutorial 3 PDF p.3, P4; solution notebook cell 7. Arrange these actions: choose a language member depending on p; choose exponent i; assume regularity; consider every valid decomposition; obtain p; show a pumped nonmember. Identify who controls p, s, the split and i. > [!tip]- Hint > An existentially promised split may be chosen helpfully for the purported regular language, not conveniently for your counterexample. > [!success]- Solution > Order: assume regularity → obtain the promised p → choose $s\in L$, $|s|\ge p$ → consider an arbitrary legal split $s=xyz$, $|xy|\le p$, $|y|>0$ → choose i≥0, possibly depending on that split → prove $xy^iz
otin L$. > > The lemma supplies p; the prover chooses s; an adversary may choose any legal split; the prover chooses i last. We must defeat every such split. Quantified refutation: for each proposed p, exhibit a member s for which every legal split has some failing exponent. T3 P5 — Diagnose both proposed proofs ORIGINAL COURSE EXERCISE: Tutorial 3 PDF pp.3–4, P5, solution notebook cell 8. For $L=\{0^n1^n:n\ge0\}$, judge each argument, identify/explain its invalid step and repair it. A: assume p, choose $s=0^p1^{p+1}$ because its length is at least p, take a legal split, infer $y=0^t$, pump down to $0^{p-t}1^{p+1}
otin L$, conclude nonregularity. B: choose $s=0^p1^p$, choose $x=\varepsilon,y=0,z=0^{p-1}1^p$, pump down to $0^{p-1}1^p
otin L$, conclude nonregularity. > [!tip]- Hint > Audit the witness's membership before auditing decomposition ownership. > [!success]- Solution > A is invalid: $0^p1^{p+1}
otin L$. The lemma only promises a pumpable split for sufficiently long members, not arbitrary long inputs. Correcting its length alone is insufficient. > > B is incomplete: its chosen split is legal but not the only legal split. A regular language might have a different split that pumps forever. > > Repair both: assume p≥1; choose $s=0^p1^p\in L$, length 2p≥p. Let any legal split have $x=0^a,y=0^t,z=0^{p-a-t}1^p$, with $a\ge0,t\ge1,a+t\le p$. Pump i=0 to obtain $0^{p-t}1^p$, outside L because counts differ. This holds for every permitted a,t and contradicts the promised split. Hence L is nonregular. T3 P6 — Similar definitions, different languages ORIGINAL COURSE EXERCISE: Tutorial 3 PDF pp.4–5, P6.1–2; solution notebook cell 9. For some k≥1, define $B=\{1^kw:w\in\{0,1\}^*	ext{ contains at least k ones}\}$ and $C=\{1^kw:w\in\{0,1\}^*	ext{ contains at most k ones}\}$. (1) Find a simple regex for B. (2) Give a pumping proof that C is not regular. Interpretation disclosed: k is existentially chosen per word, matching the supplied solution; a single fixed global k would change the task. > [!tip]- Hint > For B, can k=1 represent every word that has any admissible k? For C, use a separator 0 so the first block cannot absorb the later ones. > [!success]- Solution > 1 — Both inclusions for B. If $s=1^kw$ and w contains at least k ones, the first symbol is 1 and at least one later 1 exists. Thus s is denoted by $1(0\cup1)^*1(0\cup1)^*$. Conversely, a word denoted by that expression has a first 1 and a suffix with at least one 1; choose k=1. This proves the regex equality. > > 2 — Witness for C. Assume regularity and obtain p≥1. Take $s=1^p01^p\in C$ with k=p and length 2p+1≥p. > > Every split: $y=1^t$, $1\le t\le p$, since $|xy|\le p$. Pump i=0 to get $s'=1^{p-t}01^p$. > > Every possible new representation: any admissible k' must be at most p−t; the suffix then contains at least p ones, so it cannot contain at most k'. If p=t, s' begins with 0 and no k'≥1 exists. Thus s' is not in C for every split. Contradiction: C is not regular. > > The source's final “k>p” is stronger than necessary; the actual membership constraint would require k≥p, incompatible with k≤p−t > The optional notebook experiment compiles B's regex, not C; finite software checks cannot prove C's nonregularity.
